{{NZelectorateShortDesc|type=general|current=yes|region=Canterbury}}
'''Rangitata''' is an [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] in the [[South Island]] of New Zealand. It first existed for two parliamentary terms in the late 19th century and was re-established for the [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008 general election]]. It largely replaced the [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki electorate]], but included parts of the [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia electorate]] as well.

It is held by [[James Meager]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National]] Party following the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].  Previously it was held by [[Jo Luxton]] of the [[Labour Party of New Zealand|Labour]] Party following the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], and [[Andrew Falloon]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National]] Party from the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 election]]. Rangitata was a relatively safe National seat until it changed to the [[Labour Party of New Zealand|Labour]] Party in the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]]. Following the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]], the [[New Zealand National Party]] regained the seat.

The electorate includes both rural areas, and towns such as [[Timaru]] and [[Temuka]], these two towns being Labour strongholds giving the electorate a different composition to its more conservative neighbours [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn]] and [[Waitaki (New Zealand electorate)|Waitaki]].

==Population centres==
In the 1887 electoral redistribution, although the [[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Representation Commission]] was required through the Representation Act 1887 to maintain existing electorates "as far as possible", rapid population growth in the [[North Island]] required the transfer of three seats from the [[South Island]] to the north. Ten new electorates were created, including Rangitata, and one former electorate was recreated.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=50–53}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Rangitata.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The boundaries of the Rangitata electorate were adjusted in the 2013/14 review, when an area around [[Rakaia]] was transferred to the Selwyn electorate.<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=26 September 2014 |page=10 |date=4 April 2014}}</ref> The boundary shifted eastwards again in the 2019/20 review, when the boundary was aligned with the [[Rakaia River]].

The current electorate includes the following population centres (with approximate populations in brackets):
* [[Ashburton, New Zealand|Ashburton]] (20,000)
* [[Methven, New Zealand|Methven]] (1,300)
* [[Temuka]] (4,000)
* [[Timaru]] (42,800)

==History==
The Rangitata electorate was first established for the {{NZ election link|1887}}.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=163}}  The election was contested by [[William Rolleston]], who had represented {{NZ electorate link|Geraldine}} in the previous Parliament,  [[Searby Buxton]], and William Palmer.<ref name="Rangitata nomination, 1887">{{cite news |title=Rangitata |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=CHP18870917.2.36.8 |access-date=19 May 2012|work=[[The Press]] |volume=XLIV |issue=6859 |date=17 September 1887|page=6}}</ref> Buxton beat Rolleston, who had been in Parliament since 1868, by 588 to 507 votes, with Palmer receiving 14 votes.<ref name="Rangitata election, 1887">{{cite news |title=Rangitata |url= https://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=AS18870928.2.18.6 |access-date=19 May 2012 |work=[[Auckland Star]]|volume=XVIII |issue=228 |date=28 September 1887|page=5}}</ref>{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=231}} Buxton held the electorate until the end of the term in 1890,{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=99}} when the electorate was abolished again.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=163}}

The electorate was re-established for the {{NZ election link|1893}}{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=163}} and won by [[William Maslin]], who served until the end of the parliamentary term in 1896.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=1253}} The electorate was again abolished at the end of that term.{{sfn|Scholefield|1950|p=163}}

The present electorate, established for the {{NZ election link|2008}}, consists of the old [[Aoraki (New Zealand electorate)|Aoraki electorate]], and those parts of the old [[Rakaia (New Zealand electorate)|Rakaia electorate]] not included in the [[Selwyn (New Zealand electorate)|Selwyn electorate]].

It was created after a review of electoral boundaries conducted in the wake of the [[2006 New Zealand census|2006 census]], which showed that there had been a general northwards population movement. Even though the number of South Island electorates was fixed, the decline in the population of electorates from Rakaia south resulted in the boundaries of electorates from {{NZ electorate link|Invercargill}} north to Rakaia shifting northwards. At the time of the formation of the Rangitata electorate, [[Jo Goodhew]] was the incumbent in the Aoraki electorate.<ref name="parliamentary profile">{{cite web |title=Hon Jo Goodhew |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/current/51MP2321/goodhew-hon-jo |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=30 September 2014 |date=23 September 2014}}</ref> She held the Rangitata electorate since its creation and in the {{NZ election link|2014}}, she more than doubled her majority.<ref name="parliamentary profile" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Montgomerie |first1=Jack |title=Goodhew, Dean back with bigger majorities |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/10527541/Goodhew-Dean-back-with-bigger-majorities |access-date=30 September 2014 |work=[[The Timaru Herald]] |date=22 September 2014}}</ref>

Goodhew announced in January 2017 that she would not be contesting the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]] after being dropped from cabinet.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/national-mp-jo-goodhew-quits-after-being-dumped-from-cabinet/CLMJ6TVX2YPCSMDNZ7WFP5RJG4/ |access-date=27 September 2017|title=National MP Jo Goodhew quits after being dumped from Cabinet|date=25 January 2017|work=[[The New Zealand Herald]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031033101/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/national-mp-jo-goodhew-quits-after-being-dumped-from-cabinet/CLMJ6TVX2YPCSMDNZ7WFP5RJG4/|archive-date=31 October 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> The electorate was won at the election by [[Andrew Falloon]], retaining it for the National Party.<ref>{{cite web |title=Rangitata – Official Result 2017 general elections |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/electorate-details-42.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=20 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200115202341/https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2017/electorate-details-42.html |archive-date=15 January 2020}}</ref>

In mid-July 2020, Falloon announced that he would not be seeking re-election at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020 general election]], citing personal reasons following the suicide of a friend.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12349451 |title=National MP Andrew Falloon quits amid 'significant mental health issues' |date=20 July 2020 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=20 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200720075633/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12349451|archive-date=20 July 2020}}</ref> Following reports that Falloon had sent pornographic images to several women, he resigned as the Member for Rangitata under pressure from National Party leader [[Judith Collins]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Cooke |first1=Henry |title=Andrew Falloon sent sexually explicit photos to another young woman |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300061529/andrew-falloon-sent-sexually-explicit-photos-to-another-young-woman |access-date=20 July 2020 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=21 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200720222603/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300061529/andrew-falloon-sent-sexually-explicit-photos-to-another-young-woman |archive-date=20 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12349538 |title=New sex-text claims: National MP Andrew Falloon quits politics immediately; Judith Collins says she 'no longer trusts his story' |date=21 July 2020 |work= [[The New Zealand Herald]] |access-date=21 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200720221402/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=12349538|archive-date=20 July 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=O'Brien |first1=Tova |title='Pattern of behaviour' emerging: Further allegations of lewd images sent by disgraced ex-National MP Andrew Falloon |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/07/pattern-of-behaviour-emerging-further-allegations-of-lewd-images-sent-by-disgraced-ex-national-mp-andrew-falloon.html |access-date=20 July 2020 |work=[[Newshub]] |date=20 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200720222852/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2020/07/pattern-of-behaviour-emerging-further-allegations-of-lewd-images-sent-by-disgraced-ex-national-mp-andrew-falloon.html |archive-date=20 July 2020}}</ref>

On 9 August 2020, the National Party nominated Environment Canterbury councillor Megan Hands as their new candidate for Rangitata.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Comer |first1=Rachel |last2=Mohanlall |first2=Samesh |title=Megan Hands National's new Rangitata candidate |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/timaru-herald/news/300077843/megan-hands-nationals-new-rangitata-candidate |access-date=31 October 2020 |work=[[Timaru Herald]] |publisher=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |date=9 August 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200929190044/https://www.stuff.co.nz/timaru-herald/news/300077843/megan-hands-nationals-new-rangitata-candidate |archive-date=29 September 2020|url-status=live}}</ref> During the 2020 general election held on 17 October, the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]'s candidate [[Jo Luxton]] captured Rangitata from National, defeating Hands by a final margin of 4,408 votes.<ref>{{cite web |title=Rangitata – Official Result |url=https://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-40.html |publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]] |access-date=9 November 2020 }}</ref>

